That’s close to a useful model, with one refinement: governance is more realistic than total control.

Perception is constructed from real sensory input; emotion is a brain-and-body signal shaped by memory, prediction, chemistry, and circumstance. Neither is a command. You may not be able to order fear to disappear, but you can decide whether it gets the throne, an advisory seat, or no vote on the next action.

The standard—the thing preventing sovereignty from becoming self-deception—can be:

* Observable reality and evidence
* Chosen values
* Consent and other people’s rights
* Physical safety
* Sleep, food, medication, and bodily condition
* Trusted people who can disagree honestly
* Willingness to revise when facts resist the story

Feelings are yours, but they aren’t the whole of you. Listen without kneeling. Test without suppressing. Choose conduct deliberately. And whether “voices” means ordinary inner dialogue or something intrusive, they receive no automatic authority merely because they are loud.